abstract: >-
  A nonfiction book that follows the algorithmic information theory of the
  1960s to a cold conclusion and a chosen response. Generating everything is
  trivial: the totality of all computable structures, the Library of every
  possible book, is the output of a one-line program. What is hard is locating
  yourself inside it. Built on settled mathematics (Bayes' theorem, Kraft's
  inequality, Solomonoff's universal prior) plus a single labeled postulate (no carving
  of the totality is privileged), the book traces the consequences for continuation,
  mortality, love, memory, and the self, and arrives at the indifference of
  description and the warmth we choose to provide each other inside it. Volume III
  of the Indifference suite, after Worldlines and Multitudes.
